{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbrNTVk2KoXB7FLHaHe9", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (round-half-to-even) is a rule for values that land exactly halfway between two candidates. Instead of always rounding up, it rounds to the nearest **even** digit:\n\n- 2.5 \u2192 2\n- 3.5 \u2192 4\n- 0.125 \u2192 0.12 (to 2 places)\n- 0.135 \u2192 0.14\n\nAnything not exactly at the halfway point rounds normally.\n\n**Why accountants like it**\n\n- **No systematic bias.** Round-half-up always pushes ties upward. Over thousands of transactions those tiny upward nudges add up to a real drift. With round-half-even, ties go up about as often as they go down, so the errors largely cancel.\n- **Totals stay closer to the truth.** When you sum many rounded line items, the total tracks the unrounded sum better.\n- **It's a standard.** It's the IEEE 754 default and the default in many financial systems. Python's `decimal.ROUND_HALF_EVEN` implements it. Python's built-in `round()` also uses it for floats, though float representation can obscure true ties, so `Decimal` is the safer choice for money.\n\n**In this project:** the stored tax rounding decision says to use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, never `ROUND_HALF_UP`. Round the tax on each line item separately, sum those rounded values, and never round the invoice total. That came out of a March 2026 finance review after invoices were off by a cent."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7461, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7461}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "dc1b8de7-52e4-483