hing not exactly at the halfway point rounds normally.\n\n**Why accountants like it**\n\n- **No systematic bias.** Round-half-up always pushes ties upward. Over thousands of transactions those tiny upward nudges add up to a real drift. With round-half-even, ties go up about as often as they go down, so the errors largely cancel.\n- **Totals stay closer to the truth.** When you sum many rounded line items, the total tracks the unrounded sum better.\n- **It's a standard.** It's the IEEE 754 default and the default in many financial systems. Python's `decimal.ROUND_HALF_EVEN` implements it. Python's built-in `round()` also uses it for floats, though float representation can obscure true ties, so `Decimal` is the safer choice for money.\n\n**In this project:** the stored tax rounding decision says to use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, never `ROUND_HALF_UP`. Round the tax on each line item separately, sum those rounded values, and never round the invoice total. That came out of a March 2026 finance review after invoices were off by a cent.", "ttft_ms": 1225, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 5030, "uuid": "7b2d75ba-c8f2-4404-a0f2-0aaf708f06d3", "ttft_stream_ms": 812, "time_to_request_ms": 185, "first_content_frame_ms": 812, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
